{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before dawn, when the world seems to be holding its breath. The sky is not yet blue but no longer black, a soft, bruised violet that makes everything feel possible. The air is cooler then, cleaner somehow, as if the night has washed it. If you step outside, you can hear things you miss during the day: the distant hum of a delivery truck, the rustle of birds shifting in their sleep, the faint buzz of a streetlight about to click off.\n\nIn that quiet, my mind feels less crowded. The usual chatter of deadlines, notifications, and unfinished thoughts quiets down to a low murmur. It is easier to remember who you are when no one is asking you to be anything. I like to make tea in that hour and just sit with it, watching steam curl and vanish. No phone, no book, just the slow lightening of the sky.\n\nThere is a lesson there, I think. We often assume that clarity comes from effort, from pushing harder and thinking louder. But sometimes it comes from stillness, from waiting for the light to arrive on its own. The day will come regardless, with all its noise and demands. For a few minutes, though, you can exist outside of it, suspended between night and morning, belonging fully to neither and therefore free from both.\n\nAnd that freedom, however brief, is enough to carry with you into the bright, busy hours that follow ahead.",
